# Hole-Boosted Cu(Cr,M)O              <sub/>2</sub>              Nanocrystals for All-Inorganic CsPbBr              <sub/>3</sub>              Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
The all-inorganic CsPbBr3 perovskite solar cell (PSC) is a promising solution to balance the high efficiency and poor stability of state-of-the-art organic-inorganic PSCs. Setting inorganic hole-transporting layers at the perovskite/electrode interface decreases charge carrier recombination without sacrificing superiority in air. Now, M-substituted, p-type inorganic Cu(Cr,M)O2 (M=Ba2+ , Ca2+ , or Ni2+ ) nanocrystals with enhanced hole-transporting characteristics by increasing interstitial oxygen effectively extract holes from perovskite. The all-inorganic CsPbBr3 PSC with a device structure of FTO/c-TiO2 /m-TiO2 /CsPbBr3 /Cu(Cr,M)O2 /carbon achieves an efficiency up to 10.18 % and it increases to 10.79 % by doping Sm3+ ions into perovskite halide, which is much higher than 7.39 % for the hole-free device. The unencapsulated Cu(Cr,Ba)O2 -based PSC presents a remarkable stability in air in either 80 % humidity over 60 days or 80 °C conditions over 40 days or light illumination for 7 days.

## Hole-Boosted $\mathbf{Cu(Cr,M)}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ Nanocrystals for All-Inorganic CsPbBr3 Perovskite Solar Cells
Organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are undoubtedly one of the most promising energy conversion devices because the highest power conversion efficiency (PCE) has been increased to competitive $25.2\%$ in recent decade.[1–5] However, their commercialization is still a challenge by poor thermal and/or moisture stability in spite of great progresses in either setting blocking layers at interfaces or developing new encapsulation techniques.[6,7] Under longterm illumination and heat attack, the ion migration behavior in hybrid devices is arguable and difficult to completely avoid.[8] Aiming for improved stability, all-inorganic $\mathrm{(CsPb{X_{3}}}$ , $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{I}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ , or $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ ) perovskites free of volatile components by replacing the organic $\mathbf{MA}^{+}$ or $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ species with inorganic ${\bf C}{\bf s}^{+}$ have been widely studied for alternative light absorbers.[9,10] Among them, tri-brominated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ shows superior environmental tolerance even in $90\%$ humidity, but their photovoltaic performances are still sluggish owing to the substantial charge losses and dilatory photogenerated charge diffusion.[11,12] Although the energy band engineering is prevalent to accelerate charge separation,[13] the state-ofthe-art hole transporting materials (HTMs) are generally organic species, such as spiro-MeOTAD, PTAA, or P3HT, which will introduce new hazards (hygroscopic additives, or light/thermal instability) and subsequently degrade the cell efficiency,[14,15] against the establishment of all-inorganic PSCs. Therefore, how to promote the charge separation without sacrificing the excellent stability is an urgent projection for further PSC platforms.
Compared to organic HTMs, inorganic p-type materials such as $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}$ ,[16] $\mathrm{CuO}_{x}$ ,[17] CuI,[18] CuSCN,[19] and Cu-based delafossite materials $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}_{\mathrm{ABO_{2}}}$ , $\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}^{+}$ or $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{B}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{l}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathbf{Ga}^{3+})^{[20,21]}$ have been experimentally functionalized as hole extraction layers in PSCs, significantly improving the photostability especially in inverted configuration. It is noteworthy to mention that delafossite $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ with a band gap of about $3.0\mathrm{eV}$ is probably the most prospective p-type candidate because $\mathrm{Cr}$ element has the strongest influence on the valence band maximum (VBM).[22] However, the lower electrical conductivity and hole mobility always make ${\mathrm{CuCrO}}_{2}$ present a sluggish hole-transfer dynamic. A fact is that traditional oxygen intercalation to improve the electrical conductivity is not feasible owing to small lattice parameter of $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ phase for interstitial oxygen defect location stably.[23] According to previous reports, partial substitution of $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ with divalent or trivalent cations such as $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+},\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}^{2+},\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}^{2+},$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ can significantly improve the electrical conductivity and carrier concentration, with an enhanced conductivity up to $220\thinspace\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ .[24] Inspired by this, we propose the use of Msubstituted $\mathbf{\left(M=Ba^{2+}\right.}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , or $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ) $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystals as efficient HTMs in all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSCs with an architecture of fluorine-doped tin oxide $\mathrm{(FTO)}/c{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/m$ - $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C s P b B r_{3}/C u(C r,M)O_{2}/}$ carbon. By tuning the VBM edges of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystals, the optimal device achieves a champion PCE as high as 10.18 percent and demonstrates nearly unchanged performance after storing in $80\%$ humidity without encapsulation for 60 days or $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ conditions over 40 days, mainly attributing to an enhanced hole conductivity and graded energy alignment between $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and carbon similar to a bi-layered HTM.
Figure 1a shows the $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of pristine $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ and M-substituted $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystals by a conventional hydrothermal reaction. Obviously, four characteristic diffraction peaks for (006), (012), (009), and (110) planes are readily indexed to the standard patterns for the hexagonal-structured $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ crystalline phase (JCPDS No. 39-0247) in both doped and undoped samples.[25] The absence of impurity phase confirms the high purity of the nanocrystals. However, a close observation of the (006) plane reveals the characteristic peak shifts to lower-angle position upon doping $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , or $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ into the lattice with an order of $\mathrm{Cu(Cr,Ba)O_{2}\ >\ C u(C r,C a)O_{2}\ >\ C u(C r,N i)O_{2}}$ (see Figure 1b). The potential mechanism behind this phenomenon is mainly ascribed to the larger ion radius of $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{a}^{2+}\left(1.35\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ $(1.0\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ $(0.69\mathring\mathrm{A})$ in comparison to $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ $(0.615\mathring\mathrm{A})$ . According to previous reports,[26,27] the larger ions induced lattice expansion allows more interstitial oxygen atoms to be inserted into host $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ lattice, causing an improved hole conductivity, which can be cross-checked by Hall-effect measurement (Supporting Information, Figure S1). The superior hole carrier mobility is a prerequisite for high-performance PSCs, therefore inorganic $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ demonstrates great potential for enhanced hole extraction from perovskite to reduce charge carrier recombination. When subjected to transmission electron microscopy (TEM) characterization, as shown in Figure 1c and the Supporting Information, Figure S2, the $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystals display a narrow grain size distribution with an average size of $10\mathrm{nm}$ . The distance between defined lattice fringes is around $0.245\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (012) plane of $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ nanocrystals. The nearly unchanged or even reduced size of nanocrystals by doping M elements is beneficial for the formation of uniform and compact film (Supporting Information, Figure S3), and the corresponding element mapping images (Supporting Information, Figure S4) further confirm the successful introduction of ionic impurity into the $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ crystalline lattice.
To reveal the doping effect on the energy levels of ${\mathrm{CuCrO}}_{2}$ nanocrystals, we have further conducted the cyclic voltammetry characterization to ensure the VBM values (Supporting Information, Figure S5). According to the standard formula :[28] $E_{\mathrm{VBM}}{=}{-}(4.4+E_{\mathrm{ox,onset}})\:\mathrm{eV},$ where $E_{\mathrm{ox,onset}}$ represents the oxidation potential, the $E_{\mathrm{VBM}}$ values are determined to be $-5.22,\:-5.26,\:-5.32,$ and $-5.45\mathrm{eV}$ for undoped, $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ doped $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ nanocrystals, respectively. These data are consistent with a previous report;[29] the upper shift of $E_{\mathrm{VBM}}$ value upon introducing metallic ions may be attributed to the increased hole concentration near the valence band (increased oxygen vacancies), which can be also crosschecked from the increased interstitial oxygen amount obtained by $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) characterization (Figure 1 e; Supporting Information, Figure S6).
Upon introducing the $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ into the host $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ lattice, the gradually enhanced relative area of peak “ii” to peak “i” indicates the increased interstitial oxygen owing to a lattice expansion.[26] Assisted with the optical band gaps (Supporting Information, Figure S7), the corresponding conduction band minimum (CBM) levels are deduced to be about $-2.1\mathrm{eV}$ for all samples; therefore the energy level distribution is compatible to that of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ perovskite layer and carbon electrode, which is feasible to accelerate the hole extraction.
A typical $\mathrm{FTO/\itc-TiO_{2}/m-T i O_{2}/C s P b B r_{3}/C u(C r,M)O_{2}/}$ carbon structure is fabricated to study the performances of $M$ -substituted $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystals as HTMs in allinorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSCs (Figure 2a), in which the highquality perovskite film with average grain size of about $700\mathrm{nm}$ (the corresponding characterizations are provided in the Supporting Information, Figure S8) is made by the multistep solution processable technology proposed by our group.[30] Figure 2 b shows the cross-sectional scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of a $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystalcoated perovskite film with well-defined boundary between each layer (the cross-sectional SEM image of a whole device is provided in the Supporting Information, Figure S9), demonstrating that the introduction of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystals does not destroy the underlying perovskite film. The top-view SEM image of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ film with uniform and compact features is presented in Figure 2 c. It is reasonable that the carrier transfer can be conducted well across the seamlessly contacted interfaces. To optimize the PSC performance, the control device free of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystals was fabricated as a reference, and the dependence of photovoltaic performance on $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ doping dosage are carefully studied (Supporting Information, Figure S10 and Table S1), giving an optimal doping amount at about $10\%$ . Following this conclusion, we notice a further efficiency improvement by controlling the doping element species into $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ lattice from the current–voltage $(J{-}V)$ measurements. As shown in Figure $2\mathrm{d}$ , the PCE of the solar cell free of $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ is $7.39\%$ , which is comparable to our previous reports,[12,30,31] and it increases to $8.41\%$ by adding $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ layer, and further increases to $9.44\%$ , $10.03\%$ , and $10.18\%$ by incorporating $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{Ni})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{Ca})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{Ba})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ layers, respectively. The photovoltaic parameters, including short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ , open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ , fill factor (FF), and PCE, are summarized in Table 1. Obviously, the improvement of device efficiency is mainly attributed to the significantly enhanced $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and FF up to $1.602\mathrm{V}$ and $83.4\%$ for $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{Cr},B a)O_{2}}}$ -containing device. It is well known that the $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ depends on recombination rate in perovskite film.[32] The graded energy-level alignment (Figure 2 e) is beneficial for robust charge carrier collection. Furthermore, the PCE is increased to $10.79\%$ by doping $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions into $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ halide and using $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{Ba})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as a hole-transporting material (Supporting Information, Figure S12).
Comparing to sensitized solar cells or silicon solar cells, the efficiency of PSC is always deviated owing to their serious hysteresis effect under forward and back scan conditions (Supporting Information, Figure S11).[33] We note that the optimal $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSCs have reduced $J{-}V$ hysteresis, which may be ascribed to the accelerated hole extraction and reduced interfacial charge accumulation. As shown in Figure $2\mathrm{f}.$ the steady-state power output (SPO) measurement preserves a stable power-outputting response of $7.21\%$ and $10.08\%$ under the bias at maximal power point for control and champion devices, respectively. Incident photon-tocurrent efficiency (IPCE) spectra are performed to further cross-check the validity of the aforementioned efficiency enhancement (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ ), the integrated photocurrent densities agree well with the results from $J{-}V$ curves. Additionally, the statistical PCE distributions for a large batch of 10 solar cells in Figure 2 h show an increased trend along with tuning $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ properties.
To understand why the incorporation of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ materials improves the device performance, we carry out a range of recombination characterizations to probe the hole transfer dynamics at perovskite $\mathsf{\Pi}^{\prime}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ interface. Figure 3a shows the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) measurements of $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{FTO/CuCrO_{2}/C s P b B r_{3}}$ , and FTO/ $\mathrm{Cu(Cr,M)O_{2}/C s P b B r_{3}}$ films. All the films exhibit a sharp PL peak centered at about $535\mathrm{nm}$ near the band gap edge of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film, which is accordance with reports elsewhere.[34] However, there is a PL quenching behavior upon introduction of inorganic p-type materials. It is known that the PL intensity is highly dependent on the coupling dynamics of excited electrons at conduction band and holes at valence band. In this fashion, these results demonstrate a better hole-transfer efficiency from perovskite to $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , in accordance to its advantageous energy level alignment and superior charge transport property. Moreover, time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectra are further used to confirm the ideal band-edge alignment and efficient hole extraction behavior (Figure 3 b). According to the biexponential function:[35] $I=$ $A\mathrm{e}^{-(t-t_{0})/\tau_{1}}+\mathbf{B}\mathrm{e}^{-(t-t_{0})/\tau_{2}}$ , where $\tau_{1}$ refers to the faster component of trap-mediated nonradiative recombination, and $\tau_{2}$ is the slower component correlated to radiative recombination, the carrier lifetime has been significantly shortened to 1.33 ns for $\mathrm{FTO/Cu(Cr,Ba)O_{2}/C s P b B r_{3}}$ film from $4.60\mathrm{ns}$ for FTO/ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film, ensuring the superior hole extraction capability of inorganic $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ layer. In comparison with the pristine ${\mathrm{CuCrO}}_{2}$ , the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ induced oxygen vacancies are responsible for this accelerated charge extraction and reduced charge recombination. Therefore, doping larger ions into $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ lattice provides a way of realizing the hole boosted p-type inorganic materials for efficient PSCs.
When assembled into photovoltaic device, an undoubted law is that the radiative and nonradiative recombination are expected to be minimum, realizing the maximal carrier extraction and electricity output. To understand the intrinsic recombination mechanism, $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ as a function of incident light intensity $(I)$ are measured and fitted via the following equations:[36,37] $\begin{array}{l l}{{I_{\mathrm{sc}}\propto I^{\mathrm{a}},}}&{{V_{\mathrm{oc}}{=}n k T{\ln(I)}/q+\mathrm{con-}}}\end{array}$ stant, in which $k$ represents the Boltzmann constant, $T$ is absolute temperature, $q$ is the elementary charge, and $\scriptstyle a$ and $n$ represent factors related to bimolecular and monomolecular recombination, respectively. As shown in Figure 3 c,d, the nearly unchanged $a$ data suggests a negligible bimolecular recombination in photovoltaics under low light intensity (less than 100 suns),[38] while the significantly reduced $n$ demonstrates the suppressed interfacial trap-assisted recombination owing to the well-improved interface resulting from graded energy alignment.
High efficiency and improved stability are two prerequisites to forward the commercialization of PSCs.[39] We monitor the long-term stability of control and champion devices free of encapsulation under $80\%$ relative humidity at room temperature (Figure $\mathtt{4a}$ ; Supporting Information, Figure S13) or $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $0\%$ RH condition (Figure 4b; Supporting Information, Figure S14). Both devices show substantial longterm stability, maintaining nearly $100\%$ of their initial PCEs after dozens of days storage. The unprecedented moisture, thermal, and photostability suggests all-inorganic Cu$(\mathrm{Cr},\mathbf{M}){\mathbf O}_{2}$ crystals are superior in comparison with organic HTMs such as P3HT (Supporting Information, Figure S15).
In summary, we demonstrate the hole boosting of p-type $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by inserting interstitial oxygen during doping $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , or $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ into $\mathrm{CuCrO}_{2}$ lattice. $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystals are promising HTMs for high-efficiency, stabilized, all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSCs. The optimized $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ solar cell with $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{Ba}){\bf O}_{2}$ crystal achieves a champion efficiency as high as $10.18\%$ and it increases to $10.79\%$ by doping $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions for perovskite optimization, which is mainly attributed to the bridged energy alignment from perovskite to Cu$(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{Ba}){\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ and boosted transfer in $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{Ba})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ layer. Moreover, the inorganic $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{M})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanocrystals is capable to stabilize all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ PSC under the moisture, thermal, or light attacks. This work highlights a new perspective of hole-boosted inorganic nanocrystals as charge-selective materials for efficient and stable PSC platforms.
